# If the object that the base constructor makes counted as an object of the base class whatever the new target is, `new B().m()` would go straight to the method of A, the override would be left out of the executable, and the field that A's constructor stores would be unknown in B. If handing the new target on let it escape, as in the first version, the constructor of every derived class would escape
# class A { constructor() { this.x = "a"; }  m() { return "A"; } }  class B extends A { m() { return true; } }  function ask(x) { return x.m(); }  ask(new B());
# Whether the tables prove the new target of A at all is Link's choice. It is never the class A alone: the walk would fold the prototype of the new object to that of A.
